  const HOW_BIOSWALES_REPORT_16388=["<h2>How to read this</h2>\n<p><strong>If you have two minutes</strong>, read §1 and §6.0. That is the problem and the full list of what a swale does about it.</p>\n<p><strong>If you have ten</strong>, add §2, §5, and §7 — what the structure is, why this landscape used to do the job itself, and what this report does not claim.</p>\n<p><strong>If you are checking our work</strong>, §6.1 through §6.8 carry the evidence with every figure sourced and marked <strong>(measured)</strong> or <strong>(modeled)</strong>, and §6.7 lists what the evidence does <em>not</em> support. A separate Corrections Register documents claims in this field that are wrong or contested, including several we made ourselves.</p>\n<p>Nothing here is a construction drawing. Everything here requires field verification.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>§1 · What happens to rain on this land right now</h2>\n<p>When rain falls on sloped ground, most of it does not soak in. It sheets downhill, gathers into rivulets, then into channels, and leaves the property within hours. Whatever it picks up along the way — soil, fertilizer, road salt, manure — leaves with it.</p>\n<p>That water is not gone from the world. It is gone from <em>here</em>. It arrives in the nearest brook, then the nearest river, then the ocean, having spent almost no time in the ground beneath your feet.</p>\n<p>Two costs follow from this, and they arrive in opposite seasons.</p>\n<p><strong>In spring and after storms, there is too much water at once.</strong> Runoff that moves fast erodes what it crosses. It cuts gullies, undermines banks, and delivers a flood pulse downstream in a few hours instead of a few days.</p>\n<p><strong>In late summer, there is too little.</strong> A stream between rainstorms is not fed by rain. It is fed by groundwater slowly seeping into the channel from the surrounding land — a flow called <em>baseflow</em>. Land that sheds its rain quickly has little stored to give back. The well runs low, the brook runs thin, and the pasture browns first.</p>\n<p>Both problems have the same cause: the water moved too fast to be kept.</p>\n<p><strong>These are not two problems. They are one problem, arriving in two seasons.</strong></p>\n<p>Vermont saw both inside two years. July 2023 was the wettest summer on record, with catastrophic flooding. August 2025 was the driest August since 1895 — record-low streamflow and groundwater, more than four hundred families reporting dry wells in a single season, four times what the state had recorded in the previous nine years combined. Well drillers stopped taking new customers until spring.</p>\n<p>Two summers apart, in the same hills.</p>\n<hr />\n","<h2>§2 · What a bioswale is</h2>\n<p>A bioswale is a shallow, planted trench dug <em>along the contour</em> of a slope — level from end to end, so water cannot run along it. Soil excavated from the trench is mounded on the downhill side to form a low berm.</p>\n<p>That is the whole structure. No concrete, no pipe, no pump, no moving parts.</p>\n<p>Water running downhill meets the trench and stops. Held there, it does the one thing runoff never does: it waits. And while it waits, it soaks downward through the soil.</p>\n<p>The three words on this report describe the sequence:</p>\n<p><strong>Slow it.</strong> The trench interrupts downhill flow and drops the water's speed to zero. Speed is what causes erosion; removing it removes the damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Sink it.</strong> Standing water infiltrates. It passes down through soil and rock and joins the groundwater below — the saturated zone that feeds wells and springs.</p>\n<p><strong>Spread it.</strong> Because the trench is level, water spreads sideways along its full length instead of concentrating at one point. A hundred feet of swale infiltrates across a hundred feet of ground.</p>\n<p>This is old technology. Contour water-harvesting earthworks appear independently in Andean, South Asian, North African, and Southwestern Indigenous agriculture, in some cases for thousands of years. Earthline did not invent this method. It locates where the method applies.</p>\n<hr />\n","<h2>§3 · Where the water goes after it sinks</h2>\n<p>This is the part that is easy to miss, and it is the reason this report exists.</p>\n<p>Water that infiltrates does not stay under the swale. It moves — slowly, underground, downhill — along paths that can run for hundreds of meters or many kilometres, and it eventually re-emerges. It surfaces in springs, in wetlands, and in stream channels, where it becomes the baseflow that keeps water moving in dry weather.</p>\n<p>The transit is slow by design. Groundwater on longer, deeper paths spends more time in the subsurface than groundwater on short shallow ones. That delay is the entire benefit: rain that would have left in six hours instead leaves over weeks or months, arriving when the landscape needs it rather than when it can least use it.</p>\n<p>The distances involved are real and measured. On Mount Shasta in California, isotope and dissolved-gas tracing showed snowmelt infiltrating at 2,000–2,900 m elevation and discharging from springs more than 1,600 m lower, with residence times ranging from under two years at high-elevation springs to over sixty years at lower-elevation wells. Water recharged high on a landscape demonstrably supplies water low on it.</p>\n<p>In a monitored Colorado headwater catchment, modelling showed groundwater reaching the stream along long, deep flowpaths sourced from the upper catchment and its hillslopes, and accounted for roughly 16–34% of the stream's baseflow.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>§4 · Why position on the slope matters</h2>\n<p>Water intercepted near the bottom of a slope has almost no landscape left to travel through. It infiltrates and re-emerges a short distance later.</p>\n<p>Water intercepted higher up enters a longer flow path. It travels farther underground, stays stored longer, and re-emerges further down the system — supporting soil moisture, springs, wells, and stream baseflow across everything between the recharge point and the discharge point.</p>\n<p>This is why Earthline ranks corridors rather than simply counting them. Two swales of identical size do not deliver identical benefit; where they sit on the slope changes how much landscape their water passes through before it returns to the surface.</p>\n<p>The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency's assessment of headwaters makes the same point at watershed scale: headwater and seasonal streams absorb substantial rainwater, runoff, and snowmelt before flooding, and their storage capacity protects downstream communities both by moderating high flows and by maintaining flow during dry weather.</p>\n<hr />\n","<h2>§5 · This landscape used to do this by itself</h2>\n<p>Before European settlement, the streams of the northeastern United States were dammed continuously — not by people, but by beavers, in numbers difficult to imagine now. Estimates of the pre-colonial North American population range from 60 to 400 million animals, against roughly 10–15 million today; the decline is put at 80–98% of historical numbers.</p>\n<p>Their dams did precisely what a bioswale does, in the channels rather than on the hillsides: slow the water, spread it across the valley floor, and hold it long enough to soak in.</p>\n<p>The fur trade removed them. In the Northeast, harvest rates of 2,000–10,000 animals per year led to regional expatriation by the early 1700s. When the beavers went, the dams failed. One reconstruction estimates the resulting loss of pond storage between 1600 and 1840 at approximately <strong>17 million cubic metres of water and sediment</strong> — considerably more than the storage gained from all the mill dams built across the same period — and concludes that beaver eradication was a major driver of colonial-era hydrologic change.</p>\n<p>What followed is visible in the landscape today: dam failure led to stream incision, water tables dropping, and wet beaver meadows converting to drier riparian corridors.</p>\n<p>This matters for how the recommendations in this report should be read. <strong>A bioswale is not an addition to a natural system. It is a partial replacement for retention that was industrially removed from this watershed roughly four centuries ago and never restored.</strong> The comparison is not intervention versus untouched nature. It is intervention versus a landscape already stripped of most of its capacity to hold water.</p>\n<p>Beavers held the channels. Deep forest soils and their organic layers held the hillslopes. Trapping removed the first; clearing, grazing, and compaction degraded the second. Bioswales address the hillslope half — and they rebuild it, because a swale re-waters ground where native trees and vegetation can be planted back, that vegetation drops organic material that becomes mulch and then new soil, and roots and berm together retain the soil that would otherwise be washed downstream.</p>\n<hr />\n","<h2>§6 · What else a swale does</h2>\n<p>Groundwater recharge is why Earthline ranks a corridor. It is not the only thing the corridor does.</p>\n<p>Everything below is field or watershed data with the source named. Figures are marked <strong>(measured)</strong> or <strong>(modeled)</strong>. Where the evidence is weak or contested, that is stated rather than omitted.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>6.0 · The short list</h3>\n<p>What a well-sited swale does, in one line each. Every item is expanded below with its evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Water arriving too fast</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cuts the flood peak.</strong> Retention structures reduced peak flows by up to 74% in monitored field studies — the water still arrives, just later and lower.</li>\n<li><strong>Blunts flash flooding specifically.</strong> Flash floods are a rate problem, not a volume problem. A swale converts a fast surface pulse into slow subsurface travel, which is the exact intervention.</li>\n<li><strong>Lowers peak stage in the creek below.</strong> Less water arriving at once means a lower crest, less bank scour, and less channel deepening.</li>\n<li><strong>Attenuates where nothing else can.</strong> On steep rivers, gravity dominates flood-wave propagation and floodplain storage barely helps. In mountain terrain the only place to slow water is <em>upslope, before it reaches the channel.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Partly compensates for floodplains we've disconnected.</strong> Over 182,000 km of artificial levees exist in the lower 48, and blocking floodplain access raises velocity, flow, and stage both at the levee and downstream. Hillslope retention adds back storage the valley no longer has.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Water leaving too fast</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Recharges groundwater</strong> — the wells, springs, and dry-season stream flow that a landscape lives on.</li>\n<li><strong>Extends baseflow into late summer</strong> by converting a six-hour runoff event into weeks or months of slow subsurface release.</li>\n<li><strong>Works through frozen ground.</strong> In cold climates, 45–100% of depression-focused recharge occurred while the soil was still partly frozen.</li>\n<li><strong>Captures snowmelt that would otherwise run off.</strong> Ground with intact root structure sheds roughly <em>half</em> as much snowmelt as compacted ground.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The land itself</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Keeps soil on the slope.</strong> Measured soil-loss reductions of up to 96% on contour earthworks.</li>\n<li><strong>Builds new soil.</strong> Re-watered ground supports planting; planting drops organic material; organic material becomes mulch and then soil. The structure improves every year instead of degrading.</li>\n<li><strong>Reforests.</strong> A swale makes tree establishment viable on ground too dry or too eroded to support it — and the trees then do the hydrological work.</li>\n<li><strong>Installs permanent underground plumbing.</strong> Tree roots build macropores

